{
    "name": "LDR Night Light (Dark-Activated)",
    "description": "A dark-activated night light: an LDR divider (dusk/dawn modeled as the LDR resistance sweeping 1k→100k→1k via its electrical equivalent) feeds a Schmitt comparator that drives a power LED through an NPN transistor. In \"daylight\" the LED is fully dark (nA); at dusk it snaps ON to 19.5mA in a single clean transition (zero chatter — exactly one on and one off crossing in the whole run) and turns off at dawn at a different threshold (hysteresis). The BJT saturates properly (Vce≈0.08V). (ngspice-validated.)",
    "tags": ["sensor", "light", "ldr", "night-light", "led", "schmitt", "bjt"],
    "analysisConfig": {
        "analysis": {
            "type": "tran",
            "stopTime": "26",
            "stepTime": "10m"
        },
        "probes": ["v(sense)", "v(inp)", "v(ref)", "v(out)", "v(base)", "v(col)", "v(lux)", "i(R7)"]
    },
